Rachel Elizabeth Dillon <rachel@akrasiac.org> writes:
> On 2007-06-14, Dylan O'Donnell <psmithnews@spod-central.org> wrote:
>> It's that last criterion that arch-liches and master liches will
>> always fail on; they're covetous, and covetous monsters can't be
>> tamed. (They will be made peaceful, though.)
>
> This statement struck me as odd, since I thought that I had had tame
> arch-liches before. I did some testing, and it turns out that you can't
> tame an existing arch-lich, but you can polymorph a tame creature into
> an arch-lich. This seems to go against what I always took for granted
> from Eva's spoiler:
>
> "The following monsters appear to be untameable for technical reasons:
> they have their own special structures and cannot also have the structure
> for a pet. They can be made peaceful, and indeed some of them are peaceful
> by default. [list including master and arch-liches]"
>
> (cited from http://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~eva/slashem/pets.html)
>
> Before I go and dive deep into the source to try to puzzle this one out, 
> does anyone know what's going on here?

While all monster types with special extension structures (minions,
shopkeepers, vault guards, priests) are untameable for that reason, that
doesn't cover the gamut of untameable monsters; humans and covetous
monsters are there for, presumably, other unspecified reasons.

(Eva most likely has taken a comment applying only to the immediately
following line as applying to the whole rest of the conditional; the
context is ambiguous.)

Master liches and arch-liches are alone in being M3_COVETOUS while not
being M2_NOPOLY; most covetous monsters are uniques. 

